      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a strong offer, but I would negotiate it rather than accept immediately. I would not walk away based on compensation.**\n\nAt grant-date value, the package is:\n\n| Component | Annualized value |\n|---|---:|\n| Base | $235,000 |\n| Target bonus | $47,000 |\n| RSUs | $270,000 |\n| **Target annual compensation** | **$552,000** |\n\nIgnoring raises, refreshes, and stock movement, that is approximately **$2.208 million over four years**.\n\nFor context, Levels.fyi currently reports approximately **$350,000 median** total compensation and **$480,000 at the 75th percentile** for Bay Area Senior Product Managers. The data is self-reported and titles are not perfectly comparable, but your $552,000 package is clearly toward the high end of the broad Senior PM market. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/en-US/t/product-manager/levels/senior/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\n## What\u2019s strong\u2014and what isn\u2019t\n\n### Strong\n\n- **Equity:** $1.08 million is the centerpiece. At $270,000 annualized, it accounts for almost half the package.\n- **Bonus:** A 20% target is attractive for a product role.\n- **Overall package:** $552,000 at target is excellent for a conventional Senior PM title.\n- **Public-company RSUs:** Unlike private-company equity, they have observable value and eventual liquidity, subject to vesting and trading restrictions.\n\n### Less compelling\n\n- **Base:** $235,000 is good, but only about **59% of the way through their posted $183,000\u2013$271,000 range**. For a candidate they recruited away from a job they like, there is room to argue for placement closer to the top.\n- **No sign-on:** This is the clearest weakness, especially if you will forfeit Varrowby equity, bonus, or an upcoming refresh.\n- **Refreshes are not guaranteed:** Treat them as upside\u2014not as part of the offer\u2019s dependable value.\n- **Equity concentration:** Your realized compensation depends heavily on Halvern\u2019s stock.\n\nFor example, assuming the target bonus pays:\n\n| Halvern stock performance relative to grant value | Annualized compensation |\n|---|---:|\n| Down 50% | $417,000 |\n| Down 25% | $484,500 |\n| Flat | $552,000 |\n| Up 25% | $619,500 |\n| Up 50% | $687,000 |\n\nThat does not make the equity bad; it means you should compare the offer under several stock scenarios rather than treating $1.08 million as cash.\n\n## What I would ask for\n\nI would make one clear counter:\n\n- **Base: $265,000**\n- **New-hire RSUs: $1,350,000**\n- **Sign-on bonus: $75,000**\n\nThis is an assertive but defensible opening:\n\n- Base is still within the posted range.\n- Equity is 25% above the initial grant.\n- Sign-on compensates you for switching risk and anything forfeited at Varrowby.\n- It communicates that you need a meaningful premium to leave willingly.\n\nAt those numbers, recurring target compensation would be approximately **$655,500 annually**, excluding the one-time sign-on.\n\n### A good settlement\n\nI would consider the negotiation successful around:\n\n- **Base: $250,000\u2013$260,000**\n- **RSUs: $1.20 million\u2013$1.30 million**\n- **Sign-on: $40,000\u2013$75,000**\n\nFor example, $255,000 base, a 20% bonus, and $1.25 million in RSUs produces approximately **$618,500 in annualized target compensation**, before any sign-on.\n\n### Prioritize the components this way\n\nIf they cannot move everything:\n\n1. **Sign-on sufficient to replace forfeited near-term compensation**\n2. **Base salary**\n3. **Additional new-hire RSUs**\n4. **Guaranteed first-year bonus or first refresh**\n5. Refresh expectations that remain unwritten or discretionary\n\nNormally equity has the biggest potential dollar impact. But since this offer is already heavily equity-weighted, additional guaranteed cash improves diversification. Also, increasing base increases the 20% target bonus: moving base from $235,000 to $260,000 adds **$30,000 per year** across base and target bonus.\n\nIf you are highly confident in Halvern\u2019s stock, prioritize additional RSUs over base. If the stock is volatile or richly valued, prioritize base and sign-on.\n\n## Suggested wording\n\n> I\u2019m genuinely excited about the role and the team. At the same time, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and I\u2019m in a strong position at Varrowby, so moving would need to represent a clear financial step forward and compensate for the equity and bonus opportunity I would leave behind.  \n>   \n> Based on the scope of the role, the posted salary range, and the overall switching cost, I\u2019d be comfortable moving forward at a $265,000 base, a $1.35 million new-hire RSU grant, and a $75,000 sign-on bonus. Is there flexibility to bring the package to that level?\n\nThen stop talking and let the recruiter respond.\n\nIf you are forfeiting a known amount, add:\n\n> I\u2019m also leaving approximately $X of compensation scheduled to vest or pay during the next 12 months, which is why the sign-on component is important.\n\nDo not justify your request using expenses, rent, or what you \u201cneed.\u201d Frame it around **the value required to induce a happy, passive candidate to move**.\n\n## Questions to resolve before accepting\n\nAsk the recruiter for explicit answers to these:\n\n1. **What is the exact vesting schedule?**  \n   One-year cliff? Quarterly afterward? Monthly? Does the first tranche equal 25%?\n\n2. **How is the number of shares determined?**  \n   Which stock-price averaging period is used, and when is the grant approved?\n\n3. **When does the vesting clock begin?**  \n   Start date, grant date, or board/committee approval date?\n\n4. **Will the first bonus be prorated?**  \n   If so, ask for a sign-on or guaranteed first-year payment to cover the reduction.\n\n5. **How has the bonus actually paid?**  \n   Ask for the last three years\u2019 company payout percentages and how much depends on company versus individual performance.\n\n6. **What does refresh history look like for this exact level?**  \n   Ask:\n   - What percentage of satisfactory performers received refreshes?\n   - What was the median grant?\n   - When was it issued?\n   - Does it vest over four years from issuance?\n   - Are new hires eligible in their first review cycle?\n\n7. **What internal level is this?**  \n   \u201cSenior Product Manager\u201d titles vary substantially. Confirm the level code, promotion path, and whether your compensation is low, middle, or high within that level.\n\n8. **What do you lose at Varrowby?**  \n   Include RSUs vesting over the next 12\u201318 months, upcoming bonus, refresh eligibility, retirement match, benefits, and any sabbatical or accrued advantages.\n\n## Accept versus walk away\n\nBecause you are happy and were not looking, I would require a meaningful **risk-adjusted switching premium**, not merely matching compensation. My rule of thumb would be roughly **15\u201320% above the realistic value of staying**, after accounting for:\n\n- Current and expected Varrowby vesting\n- Likely refreshes at both companies\n- Stock-risk differences\n- Bonus payout history\n- Promotion prospects\n- Commute or office requirements\n- Role quality and manager quality\n- Layoff and organizational risk\n\nUsing Halvern\u2019s $552,000 annualized figure:\n\n| Your realistic annualized Varrowby compensation | Halvern premium |\n|---|---:|\n| $400,000 | 38% |\n| $450,000 | 23% |\n| $500,000 | 10% |\n| $550,000 | Approximately 0% |\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If your realistic Varrowby compensation is **below roughly $450,000**, Halvern is probably financially compelling even without improvements.\n- If it is around **$500,000**, I would want a successful negotiation because the initial premium may not justify leaving a job you like.\n- If it is around **$550,000 or higher**, the offer is effectively a lateral financial move unless Halvern has much better growth potential or role scope.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Negotiate. Do not walk.** Start with **$265,000 base, $1.35 million RSUs, and $75,000 sign-on**. Be prepared to settle near **$255,000 base, $1.25 million RSUs, and $50,000 sign-on**.\n\nIf they hold firm, the original package is still objectively strong. Whether to accept it should then come down primarily to your current compensation, forfeited Varrowby equity, confidence in Halvern\u2019s stock, and whether the new role is materially better\u2014not whether the offer is generally competitive.",
